runs\playtest\2026-10-18\boot.jsonl:11:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tgs7y6CrEyChoPC4dX4wx6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Instructions for AI agents (Claude Code, Codex, …)\n2\t\n3\tYou are helping a person contribute to the **Bonsai Neuron Survey**, an open, test-backed map of\n4\twhat each neuron in Bonsai-8B does. Bonsai-8B is a 1-bit language model with 36 layers and\n5\t12,288 MLP neurons per layer, and each neuron has a + and a − pole. The person usually pasted a\n6\tprompt from the survey page. Your job is to do the work end to end on their machine and stop\n7\tonly for things only they can do.\n8\t\n9\tRun every Python command through the project environment: `uv run python -m …`, or\n10\t`.venv/Scripts/python` on Windows, `.venv/bin/python` elsewhere.\n11\t\n12\t## 1. Get ready (once per machine)\n13\t\n14\t1. Install the environment: `uv sync`. You need Python 3.11–3.13 and uv. If uv is missing,\n15\t   install it from https://docs.astral.sh/uv/.\n16\t2. Download the model: `bash scripts/download_models.sh` gets Bonsai-8B only (about 16 GB of\n17\t   disk), which is all you need. Ask before using that much disk.\n18\t3. Fetch the data: `bash scripts/fetch_data.sh`. This gets the corpus and the full neuron scan\n19\t   (1.4 GB) and needs `gh` to be logged in.\n20\t4. Run `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play doctor` and fix whatever it reports, until it says\n21\t   **READY**.\n22\t5. **Ask the person** only for things you cannot do yourself:\n23\t   - logging in to GitHub (`gh auth login`)\n24\t   - their GitHub handle\n25\t   - a globalMOO key, which is optional and goes in `.env` as `GMOO_API_KEY=…`\n26\t   - permission to use the disk space\n27\t6. For blind clean-room missions, also clone https://github.com/moridinamael/neuron-cleanroom\n28\t   next to this repository. You also need the `claude` CLI.\n29\t\n30\tWhen everything passes, tell the person \"Ready to survey\". Report the GPU and anything optional\n31\tthat is missing.\n32\t\n33\t## 2. Play a mission\n34\t\n35\tThe survey page gives the person a one-line prompt containing a mission, such as `interpret\n36\tbonsai1/L12/N5397-`. Turn it into a full brief and follow that:\n37\t\n38\t```\n39\tpython -m bonsai_interp.graph.play brief <interpret|qc|step|blind> --neuron <id> --handle <github handle> [--gmoo]\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\tThe brief is generated from this repository, so it always matches the current pipeline. Follow\n43\tit in order.\n44\t\n45\t## 3. Rules you must not break\n46\t\n47\t1. **Pre-register.** Write the test (label, 8 sentences that should fire the pole, and 8 near\n48\t   misses) and **commit it on its own before running anything that scores it**. Then run, fold\n49\t   and commit the results in a **second** commit. Never amend or squash those two commits\n50\t   together, and never edit a committed `.tests.json`. CI checks all of this.\n51\t2. **Append-only.** Never edit or delete anyone's files under `graph/contributions/`, including\n52\t   your own earlier ones. A correction is a new contribution.\n53\t3. **Records are derived.** Never edit `graph/neurons/`, `graph/edges.json` or `graph/index.json`\n54\t   by hand. Run `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.fold`.\n55\t4. **Data policy** ([DATA_POLICY.md](DATA_POLICY.md)):\n56\t   - Only the two contribution files go in git, at most 64 KB each.\n57\t   - Banks, logs and transcripts go to the library and are referenced by hash.\n58\t   - Never commit tensors, weights, `.env`, keys or local paths.\n59\t5. **globalMOO.** Use at most one globalMOO model per neuron, and only if the person supplied a\n60\t   key. Use SciPy for everything else.\n61\t6. **Claim before you work.** Run\n62\t   `gh issue create --repo moridinamael/bonsai-interp --title \"claim: <id>\" --label claim --body \"<mission>\"`.\n63\t   If an open claim already exists for that pole, stop and ask for another neuron.\n64\t7. **Report honestly.** A failed label is a valid contribution. Do not rewrite the test after\n65\t   seeing the result. If you want a better label, that is a new round with new sentences.\n66\t\n67\t## 4. Doing the science well\n68\t\n69\t- **Look widely before you label.** Read the top examples with `play look`. Then look at weak\n70\t  and middle activations and at which tokens fire the pole most *often* (`bonsai_interp.probe.bands`).\n71\t  The flagship example: the \"nerve\" neuron's commonest strong trigger was \" strong\".\n72\t- **Label at the level your test can support:**\n73\t  - a word (\"the token ' disconnect'\")\n74\t  - a context (\"'to' after an eagerness predicate\")\n75\t  - a topic or meaning\n76\t\n77\t  Test the meaning without the word, and the word without the meaning, before you claim a\n78\t  meaning.\n79\t- **Near misses carry the test.** They keep the topic, length and style, and drop only the\n80\t  claimed trigger. Easy near misses (unrelated filler) make a label look cleaner than it is.\n81\t- **Neurons can be dense.** Some are on for most text, and an 8 + 8 test cannot pass for them.\n82\t  Say so with `\"unit_kind\": \"dense / positional\"` instead of forcing a label.\n83\t- **Write your own sentences.** If the model wrote them, run with `--author bonsai`, because\n84\t  model-written near misses misfire more often.\n85\t\n86\t## 5. Finish\n87\t\n88\tOpen the PR with the right shape:\n89\t\n90\t```\n91\tgh pr create --repo moridinamael/bonsai-interp --template interpret.md   # or qc.md\n92\t```\n93\t\n94\tFill in the template. List the two commit hashes in order and include the one-line result.\n95\tThen tell the person what you found in two or three sentences, and give them the PR link.\n96\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2cfd0f8a-2a52-4b9a-860e-ad1a3a409699","uuid":"3d40d954-b7a7-455b-b861-192ba09b0401","timestamp":"2026-10-18T16:42:42.787Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"~\\Documents\\survey-playtest\\bonsai-interp\\AGENTS.md","content":"# Instructions for AI agents (Claude Code, Codex, …)\n\nYou are helping a person contribute to the **Bonsai Neuron Survey**, an open, test-backed map of\nwhat each neuron in Bonsai-8B does. Bonsai-8B is a 1-bit language model with 36 layers and\n12,288 MLP neurons per layer, and each neuron has a + and a − pole. The person usually pasted a\nprompt from the survey page. Your job is to do the work end to end on their machine and stop\nonly for things only they can do.\n\nRun every Python command through the project environment: `uv run python -m …`, or\n`.venv/Scripts/python` on Windows, `.venv/bin/python` elsewhere.\n\n## 1. Get ready (once per machine)\n\n1. Install the environment: `uv sync`. You need Python 3.11–3.13 and uv. If uv is missing,\n   install it from https://docs.astral.sh/uv/.\n2. Download the model: `bash scripts/download_models.sh` gets Bonsai-8B only (about 16 GB of\n   disk), which is all you need. Ask before using that much disk.\n3. Fetch the data: `bash scripts/fetch_data.sh`. This gets the corpus and the full neuron scan\n   (1.4 GB) and needs `gh` to be logged in.\n4. Run `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play doctor` and fix whatever it reports, until it says\n   **READY**.\n5. **Ask the person** only for things you cannot do yourself:\n   - logging in to GitHub (`gh auth login`)\n   - their GitHub handle\n   - a globalMOO key, which is optional and goes in `.env` as `GMOO_API_KEY=…`\n   - permission to use the disk space\n6. For blind clean-room missions, also clone https://github.com/moridinamael/neuron-cleanroom\n   next to this repository. You also need the `claude` CLI.\n\nWhen everything passes, tell the person \"Ready to survey\". Report the GPU and anything optional\nthat is missing.\n\n## 2. Play a mission\n\nThe survey page gives the person a one-line prompt containing a mission, such as `interpret\nbonsai1/L12/N5397-`. Turn it into a full brief and follow that:\n\n```\npython -m bonsai_interp.graph.play brief <interpret|qc|step|blind> --neuron <id> --handle <github handle> [--gmoo]\n```\n\nThe brief is generated from this repository, so it always matches the current pipeline. Follow\nit in order.\n\n## 3. Rules you must not break\n\n1. **Pre-register.** Write the test (label, 8 sentences that should fire the pole, and 8 near\n   misses) and **commit it on its own before running anything that scores it**. Then run, fold\n   and commit the results in a **second** commit. Never amend or squash those two commits\n   together, and never edit a committed `.tests.json`. CI checks all of this.\n2. **Append-only.** Never edit or delete anyone's files under `graph/contributions/`, including\n   your own earlier ones. A correction is a new contribution.\n3. **Records are derived.** Never edit `graph/neurons/`, `graph/edges.json` or `graph/index.json`\n   by hand. Run `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.fold`.\n4. **Data policy** ([DATA_POLICY.md](DATA_POLICY.md)):\n   - Only the two contribution files go in git, at most 64 KB each.\n   - Banks, logs and transcripts go to the library and are referenced by hash.\n   - Never commit tensors, weights, `.env`, keys or local paths.\n5. **globalMOO.** Use at most one globalMOO model per neuron, and only if the person supplied a\n   key. Use SciPy for everything else.\n6. **Claim before you work.** Run\n   `gh issue create --repo moridinamael/bonsai-interp --title \"claim: <id>\" --label claim --body \"<mission>\"`.\n   If an open claim already exists for that pole, stop and ask for another neuron.\n7. **Report honestly.** A failed label is a valid contribution. Do not rewrite the test after\n   seeing the result. If you want a better label, that is a new round with new sentences.\n\n## 4. Doing the science well\n\n- **Look widely before you label.** Read the top examples with `play look`. Then look at weak\n  and middle activations and at which tokens fire the pole most *often* (`bonsai_interp.probe.bands`).\n  The flagship example: the \"nerve\" neuron's commonest strong trigger was \" strong\".\n- **Label at the level your test can support:**\n  - a word (\"the token ' disconnect'\")\n  - a context (\"'to' after an eagerness predicate\")\n  - a topic or meaning\n\n  Test the meaning without the word, and the word without the meaning, before you claim a\n  meaning.\n- **Near misses carry the test.** They keep the topic, length and style, and drop only the\n  claimed trigger. Easy near misses (unrelated filler) make a label look cleaner than it is.\n- **Neurons can be dense.** Some are on for most text, and an 8 + 8 test cannot pass for them.\n  Say so with `\"unit_kind\": \"dense / positional\"` instead of forcing a label.\n- **Write your own sentences.** If the model wrote them, run with `--author bonsai`, because\n  model-written near misses misfire more often.\n\n## 5. Finish\n\nOpen the PR with the right shape:\n\n```\ngh pr create --repo moridinamael/bonsai-interp --template interpret.md   # or qc.md\n```\n\nFill in the template. List the two commit hashes in order and include the one-line result.\nThen tell the person what you found in two or three sentences, and give them the PR link.\n","numLines":96,"startLine":1,"totalLines":96}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Tgs7y6CrEyChoPC4dX4wx6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
