s":24,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**8 of 16 held.** PR: https://github.com/moridinamael/bonsai-interp/pull/13\n\nThe cleanest results were mostly single words or narrow contexts. These held:\n- the \"ok\" inside *Avalokiteshvara*\n- \"ama\" at the end of Japanese names like Yokohama\n- the short end year of a season such as \"2013‑14\"\n- \"second\" as a rank, as in \"world's second largest\"\n- verbs of escalation such as \"intensified\" and \"widened\"\n- an open-source sharing context\n- words that open a clause, such as \"when\" and \"whether\"\n- \"In many / some …\"\n\nThe most useful failure was L34/N2482+. It fires on \" in\" before places as strongly as before dates, so a better label is probably \" in\" after a scheduling verb such as \"will open in\". L35/N10110+ (Japanese and Korean text) separated the two sets perfectly but fired too weakly to pass.\n\nThe steps went in order on branch `sweep/moridinamael-2026-10-18-1733`: pre-registration in 41ddffc, then results in 621f231. `validate` reported 0 problems, and the PR contains only contribution files.\n\nDecisions I made:\n- I marked L2/N7918− as `\"dense / positional\"` because its card flagged it as broad, with a weak maximum and the gate-closed regime. Its guess was \" nothing\", which failed.\n- I tried sentences on 10 unclear poles with `play probe`, using one model load, before writing their tests. I didn't re-test anything after the run.\n- I recorded `confidence` and `notes` on every test, and wrote all the sentences myself rather than having the model write them.\n\n**Friction**\n- **AUROC is printed wrongly.** `play run` showed L2/N7918− as \"AUROC 0.91 (need >= 0.90) … FAILED\". Recomputing it with `protocols._quick_8_8_v2` gives 0.898. The printed number and the rule that decides the outcome disagree, so the line looks self-contradictory.\n- **PowerShell couldn't run the commands.** Even a plain `git pull` was blocked as \"multiple operations\" needing approval. Git Bash worked.\n- **Windows line endings.** Writing the tests files with Python on Windows gave CRLF line endings, and `git add` printed 16 \"CRLF will be replaced by LF\" warnings. Nothing broke, but a tool that rewrites JSON could save LF itself.\n- **No helper to fill the tests files.** I had to write my own script to fill 16 files and delete `_screens_vocabulary`. A `play fill` that takes one JSON plan, like `probe --plan`, would save the most time.\n- **Optional keys aren't documented.** The brief lists `confidence`, `notes` and `unit_kind` but doesn't say what values they take. I copied the format from an earlier sweep commit.\n- **The `draw` output is long.** It prints two very long commands that each repeat all 16 paths, which adds noise; the `.sweep/latest.txt` route is enough.","ttft_ms":2031,"type":"result","duration_ms":310041,"uuid":"428b3a51-ad36-461a-8535-f592a2071109","ttft_stream_ms":1205,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":1205,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
runs\cleanroom\transcript.jsonl:5:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B2N2px3b7GVsobXuizMa17","type":"tool_result","content":"total 26\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:28 .claude\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609  106 Oct 17 21:25 .env\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:28 .git\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609   43 Oct 17 21:27 .gitignore\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 USER 197609   53 Oct 17 21:25 .venv -> ~/Documents/GitHub/monosemanticity/.venv\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609 1937 Oct 17 21:27 BRIEF.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609 7216 Oct 17 21:27 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 data\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 models\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 mono\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:26 probes\n# Brief\n\n**Target:** Bonsai-8B (1-bit), MLP neuron **layer 9, index 3688**, both poles (L9/N3688+ and L9/N3688−).\n\n**Question:** What does this neuron do? Is each pole monosemantic, meaning it does one\ninterpretable job? If not, what are its jobs?\n\nNo prior work on this neuron is available to you, and none should be sought. Work only inside\nthis folder with the toolchain in `README.md`. Do not use the web. Do not read files outside\nthis folder.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Investigate both poles with the forward, inverse, induce and circuit tools. Use your own\n  judgement about which tools and how much of each.\n- Form labels, then **try hard to break them**. Look at weak and middling activations, not\n  only the top examples. Write test sentences that could embarrass your label. Pit competing\n  hypotheses against each other.\n- **Pre-register:** write test sets and predictions to files and `git commit` them before\n  running them. Note the commit hashes in your report.\n- You may use sub-agents, for example to label examples blind or to write test sentences.\n- **Budget:** at most **one** globalMOO model in total. SciPy solvers are unlimited. Aim to\n  finish within about 3 hours of wall-clock time.\n\n## Deliverables\n\nWrite both files in this folder, then commit everything.\n\n**`REPORT.md`** should cover:\n\n- the final label for each pole, and the labels you rejected along the way, with the evidence\n  that rejected them\n- the key evidence for the final labels (numbers, example sentences, test results)\n- a monosemanticity verdict per pole: monosemantic, one main job with offshoots, several jobs,\n  or unclear. Explain why, and give your confidence.\n- what you would test next, and what you are least sure about\n\n**`summary.json`** has this shape:\n\n```\n{\"pole_plus\": {\"label\": ..., \"verdict\": ..., \"confidence\": 0..1}, \"pole_minus\": {...},\n \"rejected_labels\": [...], \"key_tests\": [{\"name\", \"prediction\", \"result\", \"commit\"}]}\n```\n# Neuron probing toolchain (Bonsai-8B, 1-bit)\n\nTools for finding out what one MLP neuron of Bonsai-8B does. The model is a 1-bit\nquantization-aware-trained Qwen3-8B variant, 36 layers, 12,288 SwiGLU channels per layer.\n\nA **neuron** is one SwiGLU channel: `silu(gate) * up` at layer L, index n. It has two\n**poles**: the positive side and the negative side. They are often unrelated jobs, so study\neach separately. **L9/N3688+** means layer 9, neuron 3688, positive pole.\n\nActivations are usually given as a **fraction of the pole's corpus maximum**. The corpus is\n8M tokens of mixed web text, code and multilingual text, and 1.0 is the strongest token in it.\n\nRun everything with the local environment:\n\n```\n.venv/Scripts/python -m <module> ...\n```\n\nSet `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8` so non-Latin text prints. The GPU fits **one model at a\ntime**, so run model-loading jobs one after another. Loading takes about 30–60 s.\n\n## Data\n\n- `data/corpus.npz`: `tokens`, shape (windows, 1024), the scanned corpus.\n- `data/scan_bonsai1.npz`: top-32 activations per pole for every neuron, with window and\n  position. `explore.py` reads it.\n\n## Tools\n\n### Forward: what text activates it\n\n- `python -m mono.explore --model bonsai1 --neuron L/n [--pole +|-] [--k 12]`\n  - Top corpus examples. The peak token is marked ⟦like this⟧.\n  - Also shows the sign regime of each example (gate × up).\n- `python -m mono.probe.run forward L/n +`\n  - Writes `probes/bonsai1_L{L}_N{n}_pos/forward.json` with the top examples of both poles.\n  - Writes `bank_request.json`, which asks for a sentence bank. You write that bank (see below).\n- `mono.probe.bands`: the full activation distribution, not just the top 32.\n  - `python -m mono.probe.bands scan --units L/n[,L/n...] --windows 6000 --tag X` records every\n    token's activation for those neurons over 6,000 windows (about 6M tokens).\n  - `bands.sample(tag, L, n, pole, k, bands=[(lo, hi), ...])` draws random contexts from\n    activation bands. Use it to look at weak and middling activations.\n  - `bands.load_bands(tag)[\"L{L}_N{n}\"]` returns the raw per-token array.\n\n### Testing text you write\n\n- `textinv.TextEvaluator(model, tok, rec, L, n, pole_max, opp_max, sign)`\n  - Call it on a list of strings. It returns, per string, `act` (this pole) and `opp`\n    (the other pole), each as a fraction of its corpus max, plus `peak_token`.\n  - Each text is read after neutral preambles. Activation is taken only on the text's own\n    tokens, because the first positions of a sequence are damped.\n  - `mono.probe.run.setup(ws)` builds one from `forward.json`.\n- `python -m mono.probe.quick L/n +` runs a pre-registered quick test from\n  `probes/<id>/quick_tests.json`:\n\n  ```\n  {\"neighbours\": [{\"unit\": \"L9/N3688\", \"pole\": \"+\", \"label\": \"...\", \"fire\": [8 sentences], \"quiet\": [8 sentences]}]}\n  ```\n\n  - A label holds if at least 5 of 8 \"fire\" sentences reach 0.3 and at most 1 \"quiet\" sentence does.\n  - It also prints the vocabulary tokens that drive the neuron directly and, if the layer is\n    above 0, the earlier neurons that feed it. Pass `with_feeders=False` to skip the feeders.\n  - It works for any neuron, so you can use it on neighbours too.\n\n### Sentence bank, dose-response and held-out tests\n\n`python -m mono.probe.run solo L/n +` needs `bank.json` in the probe folder:\n\n```\n{\"label\": {\"+\": \"...\", \"-\": \"...\"},\n \"hypotheses\": {\"rel_pos\": \"label A\", \"rel_h2\": \"label B\", ...},   # optional, competing labels\n \"concept_tokens\": [\" word\", ...], \"opposite_tokens\": [\" word\", ...], # used by induce\n \"sentences\": [{\"text\": ..., \"rel_pos\": 0..1, \"rel_neg\": 0..1, \"branch\": ..., \"kind\": \"on|near|spelling|opposite|unrelated|corpus|top\",\n                \"rel_h2\": 0..1, ...}]}\n```\n\n- Relevance ratings (0–1) give how well each sentence matches each label.\n- The stage reports, for each hypothesis, the Spearman correlation and AUROC between rating and\n  activation, plus leaks (fires off-concept) and silent on-concept sentences.\n- If `holdout.json` exists, it also scores that pre-registered held-out test:\n\n  ```\n  {\"hypothesis\": \"rel_pos\", \"sentences\": [{\"text\": ..., \"predict\": \"fire|quiet\"}]}\n  ```\n\n### Inverse: from a target activation back to text\n\n`python -m mono.probe.run invert L/n + --solver de|gmoo [--goals a,b]` searches over passages\nof 3 sentences drawn from the bank. Each sentence is placed by a (concept, strength)\ncoordinate. The goals are defined in `textinv.GOALS`:\n\n- **Levels:** hit 25 / 50 / 75% of max.\n- **max:** the strongest text in the bank's span.\n- **leak:** fire with nothing related to the label present.\n- **offshoot:** fire without the refined sense.\n- **silent_on_concept:** the concept is present but the neuron stays quiet.\n- **near_miss_ceiling:** the strongest text that is at most related.\n\nThere are two solvers:\n\n- `de`: SciPy differential evolution. Local, robust, about 600 evaluations per goal.\n- `gmoo`: globalMOO, a learned inverse model. It trains once, then answers each goal in a few\n  evaluations. It needs the key in `.env` and makes remote calls. **Use at most one globalMOO\n  model in this whole study.** Choose the problem carefully.\n\nYou can also build your own `InverseProblem`s (`mono/inverse/problem.py`) and use\n`mono/inverse/solvers.py`: `differential_evolution`, `dual_annealing`, `least_squares`,\n`lbfgs`, `GmooInverse`.\n\n### Induce: what the neuron does to the model's output\n\n`python -m mono.probe.run induce L/n +` adds c × (pole max) to the neuron during neutral\nprompts. It reports:\n\n- the tokens the neuron's output direction promotes and suppresses directly\n- p(concept_tokens) and p(opposite_tokens) as c varies\n- KL from the unsteered model\n- the tokens that gain probability\n- greedy generations at several c\n\n### Circuits\n\n`python -m mono.probe.neighbors L/n +` needs `neighbors_request.json`:\n\n```\n{\"branches\": {\"name\": [\"text\", {\"text\": ..., \"find\": \"substring of the token to read\"}]},\n \"flip\": {\"alone\": \"text where it fires\", \"flipped\": \"same text after context that silences it\", \"find\": \"token substring\"}}\n```\n\nIt reports:\n\n- **Upstream:** attribution, then zeroing each candidate alone. The share of the activation\n  lost is that neuron's input weight.\n- **Disambiguators:** for the flip pair, which upstream units push it down.\n- **Downstream:** which later neurons change when this one is zeroed.\n\nLower-level pieces:\n\n- `mono.inverse.neuron.NeuronSite`: one corpus position. Gives attribution and masked runs.\n- `mono.inverse.linear`: weights-only views (`token_weights`, `virtual_weights`).\n- `mono.model.load(\"bonsai1\")` returns `(model, tok, rec)`. `rec` is a `NeuronRecorder`:\n  - set `rec.mode = \"act\"` and `rec.layers = {L}` to record\n  - use `rec.interventions = {L: fn}` to edit activations\n  - set `rec.stop_at = L` to stop the forward pass early (raises `StopForward`)\n\n## Good practice\n\n- Write predictions and test sentences down, and `git commit` them, **before** you run them.\n- Look beyond the top examples: weak and middling activations, and how often each kind of\n  trigger occurs in the corpus.\n- Prefer tests that could embarrass your label: near misses, the concept without its usual\n  words, the usual words without the concept, other languages, competing hypotheses.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d9791a30-f86c-4ce0-b2aa-584dee454565","uuid":"17aaa07b-225e-4007-965c-5b75ef77ce19","timestamp":"2026-10-18T03:28:42.212Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 26\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:27 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:28 .claude\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609  106 Oct 17 21:25 .env\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:28 .git\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609   43 Oct 17 21:27 .gitignore\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 USER 197609   53 Oct 17 21:25 .venv -> ~/Documents/GitHub/monosemanticity/.venv\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609 1937 Oct 17 21:27 BRIEF.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 USER 197609 7216 Oct 17 21:27 README.md\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 data\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 models\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:25 mono\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 USER 197609    0 Oct 17 21:26 probes\n# Brief\n\n**Target:** Bonsai-8B (1-bit), MLP neuron **layer 9, index 3688**, both poles (L9/N3688+ and L9/N3688−).\n\n**Question:** What does this neuron do? Is each pole monosemantic, meaning it does one\ninterpretable job? If not, what are its jobs?\n\nNo prior work on this neuron is available to you, and none should be sought. Work only inside\nthis folder with the toolchain in `README.md`. Do not use the web. Do not read files outside\nthis folder.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Investigate both poles with the forward, inverse, induce and circuit tools. Use your own\n  judgement about which tools and how much of each.\n- Form labels, then **try hard to break them**. Look at weak and middling activations, not\n  only the top example