runs\playtest\2026-10-18\sweep-12-codex.jsonl:7:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play brief sweep --k 16 --handle moridinamael'","aggregated_output":"# Mission: sweep of 16 poles\r\n\r\nColour in 16 random poles that have no record yet, about 2 minutes each. One branch, one pre-registration commit,\r\none batch run, one PR. No claims: a pole someone else also tests gets two independent results, which is good.\r\n\r\n1. Branch: git checkout -b sweep/moridinamael-2026-10-18-1753\r\n2. Draw and create the tests files: uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play draw --k 16 --handle moridinamael\r\n   (writes one tests file per pole and lists them in .sweep/latest.txt; work on exactly those files,\r\n   e.g. git add --pathspec-from-file=.sweep/latest.txt and play run --list .sweep/latest.txt; both work in any shell)\r\n3. Read every card in one call: uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play card --neuron <id1> <id2> … --out cards.txt   (no GPU: examples, density, flags,\r\n   logit effect; one call loads the scan and weights once; then read cards.txt, which is not committed)\r\n   If it is unclear, try a few sentences: uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play probe --neuron <id> \"…\" \"…\"\r\n   Label at the level the card supports: usually a token or a context. A pole the card flags dense or positional\r\n   gets \"unit_kind\": \"dense / positional\" (a top-level key in its tests file) and a test of your best guess at what drives it.\r\n   It will probably fail; its job is to record the density honestly, not to find a meaning.\r\n   To try sentences on many poles with one model load: uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play probe --plan plan.json\r\n   (plan.json: {\"<pole id>\": [\"sentence\", ...], ...}). Each tests file is for ONE pole, the one in its \"neuron\" field.\r\n4. Pre-register all of them. Each pole needs a label, 8 fire sentences and 8 near misses (same topic and style, without the trigger).\r\n   Easiest: write one plan.json and run uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play fill --plan plan.json --agent \"<who you are>\"\r\n   plan.json: {\"<pole id>\": {\"label\": \"...\", \"fire\": [8 sentences], \"quiet\": [8 near misses], \"short\": \"2-5 words\",\r\n   \"unit_kind\": \"lexical | contextual | sparse feature | dense / positional | unclear\", \"confidence\": 0.6, \"notes\": \"...\"}, ...}\r\n   (fill writes LF line endings, removes the template's reference list and refuses wrong keys). Or edit the files directly:\r\n   methods.agent (who you are) and methods.screens (e.g. card, probe). quick-8+8@2 judges each: AUROC >= 0.90,\r\n   median fire >= 0.20 of the pole's max, at most 1 near miss >= 0.30.\r\n   Optional keys: notes, short, unit_kind, confidence, predictions. Delete _screens_vocabulary (it is only a reference list)\r\n   and any optional key you leave unfilled.\r\n   Commit ALL tests files together and ALONE: git add --pathspec-from-file=.sweep/latest.txt && git commit -m \"Pre-register sweep tests\"\r\n5. Run them all at once (one model load): uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play run --list .sweep/latest.txt\r\n   Commit the results SEPARATELY: git add graph/contributions && git commit -m \"Sweep results: <held>/<total> held\"\r\n6. Validate (uv run python -m bonsai_interp.graph.validate), push, and open one PR:\r\n   mkdir -p .pr && cp .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/sweep.md .pr/sweep-moridinamael-2026-10-18-1753.md (gitignored; paste the table `play run` printed), then\r\n   git push -u origin sweep/moridinamael-2026-10-18-1753 && gh pr create --repo moridinamael/bonsai-interp --title \"sweep: 16 poles (<held> held)\" --body-file .pr/sweep-moridinamael-2026-10-18-1753.md\r\n   The merge agent merges conforming PRs once the checks pass, and regenerates the map.\r\n\r\nFailed labels are results too; record them and move on. Do not re-test inside a sweep: a second round is a new mission.\r\n\r\nRules (from AGENTS.md; once CI is enabled it enforces 1-3, and play run already refuses uncommitted tests):\r\n1. Commit the .tests.json ON ITS OWN before running anything that scores it; results go in a SECOND commit. Never amend, squash or edit a committed .tests.json.\r\n2. Never edit or delete files under graph/contributions/ (yours included). Do not commit graph/neurons, edges.json or index.json: the merge agent regenerates them on main after merging.\r\n3. Only the two contribution files (<= 64 KB each) go in git; bulky material goes to the library (DATA_POLICY.md).\r\n4. Report honestly: a failed label is a valid contribution. Do not rewrite a test after seeing its result.\r\n\r\nWhen done, tell the person in two or three sentences what you found and give them the PR link.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
runs\playtest\2026-10-18\sweep-12-codex.jsonl:9:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'git status --short; git branch --show-current; Get-Content AGENTS.md; Get-Content DATA_POLICY.md; Get-Content .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/sweep.md'","aggregated_output":"main\n# Instructions for AI agents (Claude Code, Codex, â€¦)\r\n\r\nYou are helping a person contribute to the **Bonsai Neuron Survey**, an open, test-backed map of\r\nwhat each neuron in Bonsai-8B does. Bonsai-8B is a 1-bit language model with 36 layers and\r\n12,288 MLP neurons per layer, and each neuron has a + and a âˆ’ pole. The person usually pasted a\r\nprompt from the survey page. Your job is to do the work end to end on their machine and stop\r\nonly for things only they can do.\r\n\r\nRun every Python command through the project environment: `uv run python -m â€¦`, or\r\n`.venv/Scripts/python` on Windows, `.venv/bin/python` elsewhere.\r\n\r\n## 1. Get ready (once per machine)\r\n\r\nYou need about 3 GB of disk in total: 1.2 GB of weights and 1.4 GB of data. The scripts are\r\nbash; on Windows, Git Bash (which comes with Git for Windows) runs them.\r\n\r\nHardware: an NVIDIA GPU with 8 GB or more (a mission peaks at about 5 GB), or an Apple Silicon Mac (macOS 14 or later; 32 GB\r\nof unified memory recommended, 16 GB is tight). The toolchain picks the device itself (CUDA,\r\nthen Apple's MPS, then the CPU). To force one, put `BONSAI_DEVICE=cuda`, `mps` or `cpu` in\r\n`.env`. The CPU is fine for checks but too slow for missions. Mac details are in the README\r\nunder \"Running on a Mac\".\r\n\r\n1. Clone https://github.com/moridinamael/neuron-cleanroom next to this repository; blind\r\n   clean-room missions need it. Ask the person for their GitHub handle now, since every\r\n   mission uses it.\r\n2. Install the environment: `uv sync`. You need Python 3.11â€“3.13 and uv. If uv is missing,\r\n   install it from https://docs.astral.sh/uv/.\r\n3. Get the model weights (Bonsai-8B only):\r\n   - If they are **already on this machine**, put `BONSAI1_PATH=<that folder>` in `.env`\r\n     instead of downloading. The folder is the one that contains `config.json` and either the\r\n     `model-0000x-of-00004.safetensors` shards or `Bonsai-8B-Q1_0.gguf`.\r\n   - Otherwise run `bash scripts/download_models.sh`. It fetches PrismML's 1-bit GGUF (1.2 GB),\r\n     which holds exactly the same weights as the 16 GB unpacked release. A warning about unauthenticated Hugging Face requests is harmless.\r\n4. Fetch the data: `bash scripts/fetch_data.sh`. It downloads the corpus (120 MB), the full\r\n   neuron scan (1.1 GB) and a band scan (0.2 GB), and needs `gh` to be logged in.\r\n5. Run `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play doctor` and fix whatever it reports, until it says\r\n   **READY**. Doctor checks every weight shard, then loads the model and reads one known neuron.\r\n   It reports the device and its speed, and it compares the reading with the reference CUDA\r\n   machine. On a device other than CUDA, also run `uv run python tests/test_device_parity.py`\r\n   once. It checks four neurons, the model's next-token prediction and the backward pass, and it\r\n   must print PARITY OK.\r\n6. **Ask the person** only for things you cannot do yourself:\r\n   - logging in to GitHub (`gh auth login`)\r\n   - a globalMOO key, which is optional and goes in `.env` as `GMOO_API_KEY=â€¦`\r\n   - permission to use the disk space\r\n   - for blind missions, the `claude` CLI or `codex` CLI\r\n\r\nWhen everything passes, tell the person \"Ready to survey\". Report the GPU and anything optional\r\nthat is missing.\r\n\r\n## 2. Play a mission\r\n\r\nThe survey page gives the person a one-line prompt containing a mission, such as `interpret\r\nbonsai1/L12/N5397-`. Turn it into a full brief and follow that:\r\n\r\n```\r\npython -m bonsai_interp.graph.play brief <interpret|qc|step|blind> --neuron <id> --handle <github handle> [--gmoo]\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe brief is generated from this repository, so it always matches the current pipeline. Follow\r\nit in order.\r\n\r\n## 3. Rules you must not break\r\n\r\n1. **Pre-register.** Write the test (label, 8 sentences that should fire the pole, and 8 near\r\n   misses) and **commit it on its own before running anything that scores it**. Then run\r\n   and commit the results (contribution files only) in a **second** commit. Never amend or squash those two commits\r\n   together, and never edit a committed `.tests.json`. CI checks all of this.\r\n2. **Append-only.** Never edit or delete anyone's files under `graph/contributions/`, including\r\n   your own earlier ones. A correction is a new contribution.\r\n3. **PRs carry contributions only.** Never commit `graph/neurons/`, `graph/edges.json` or\r\n   `graph/index.json`: the merge agent regenerates them on `main` after each merge. You may run\r\n   `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.fold` locally to preview the record, but don't commit the output.\r\n4. **Data policy** ([DATA_POLICY.md](DATA_POLICY.md)):\r\n   - Only the two contribution files go in git, at most 64 KB each.\r\n   - Banks, logs and transcripts go to the library and are referenced by hash.\r\n   - Never commit tensors, weights, `.env`, keys or local paths.\r\n5. **globalMOO.** Use at most one globalMOO model per neuron, and only if the person supplied a\r\n   key. Use SciPy for everything else.\r\n6. **No claims.** Contributions never conflict, so nobody reserves a pole. If two people test the\r\n   same one, both results count, and the second is an independent replication.\r\n7. **Record your methods.** Fill `methods.agent` (who did the work, e.g. \"claude-code opus-5.5\") and\r\n   `methods.screens` (the analyses you used, from `bonsai_interp/graph/methods.py`) in the tests file.\r\n   `play run` adds what it can detect and the time taken. This is how the survey learns which\r\n   techniques pay off.\r\n8. **Report honestly.** A failed label is a valid contribution. Do not rewrite the test after\r\n   seeing the result. If you want a better label, that is a new round with new sentences.\r\n\r\n## 4. Doing the science well\r\n\r\n- **Explore with `play probe`.** It scores any sentences on a pole without recording anything:\r\n  `python -m bonsai_interp.graph.play probe --neuron <id> \"sentence\" â€¦`. If you write your own\r\n  scripts, run them from the repository root with `PYTHONPATH=.` so `bonsai_interp` imports.\r\n- **Start from the card.** `play card --neuron <id>` shows both poles' top examples, how dense the\r\n  pole is, its sign regimes and the tokens its output promotes, and flags dense or positional units,\r\n  in seconds and without the GPU. `play which \"text\"` goes the other way: which poles fire on each\r\n  token of your text (`--token \" nerve\"` for poles whose top examples peak on one token).\r\n- **Look widely before you label.** Read the top examples with `play look`. Then look at weak\r\n  and middle activations and at which tokens fire the pole most *often* (`bonsai_interp.probe.bands`).\r\n  The flagship example: the \"nerve\" neuron's commonest strong trigger was \" strong\".\r\n- **Label at the level your test can support:**\r\n  - a word (\"the token ' disconnect'\")\r\n  - a context (\"'to' after an eagerness predicate\")\r\n  - a topic or meaning\r\n\r\n  Test the meaning without the word, and the word without the meaning, before you claim a\r\n  meaning.\r\n- **Near misses carry the test.** They keep the topic, length and style, and drop only the\r\n  claimed trigger. Easy near misses (unrelated filler) make a label look cleaner than it is.\r\n- **Neurons can be dense.** Some are on for most text, and an 8 + 8 test cannot pass for them.\r\n  Say so with `\"unit_kind\": \"dense / positional\"` instead of forcing a label.\r\n- **Write your own sentences.** If the model wrote them, run with `--author bonsai`, because\r\n  model-written near misses misfire more often.\r\n\r\n## 5. Finish\r\n\r\nOpen the PR with the right shape:\r\n\r\n```\r\ngh pr create --repo moridinamael/bonsai-interp --template interpret.md   # or qc.md\r\n```\r\n\r\nFill in the template. List the two commit hashes in order and include the one-line result.\r\nThen tell the person what you found in two or three sentences, and give them the PR link.\r\n# What goes in the repo, what goes in the library, and what goes nowhere\r\n\r\nThe repo must stay small enough to clone in seconds while the graph grows to thousands of\r\nneurons. A quick-tested neuron costs about 16 KB here. A fully probed neuron produces 1â€“5 MB of\r\nfiles, almost all of it solver logs and generated HTML. So git holds the **evidence and the\r\nrecipe**; everything bulky lives in the **library** and is referenced by hash.\r\n\r\n## 1. In git: the evidence and the recipe\r\n\r\nEach contribution is at most two files and **64 KB per file**. A PR adds at most **256 KB under\r\n`graph/`**, and CI enforces both limits.\r\n\r\n| What | Why it is in git |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| The pre-registration (`<id>.tests.json`): label, the 8 + 8 test sentences, predictions | It *is* the test. Its commit date is the proof it came first |\r\n| The results (`<id>.json`): counts, per-sentence activation and peak token (3 decimals), outcome | Small, and what reviewers read |\r\n| Edges found while stepping out (`from`, `to`, `kind`, `weight`, `method`) | They are the graph |\r\n| The recipe: toolchain commit, command, seed | Anything here can be recomputed from it |\r\n| Up to 8 corpus examples, at most 400 characters each | Enough to see the job; corpus text stays under its own licence |\r\n| Notes, at most 4,000 characters | Longer write-ups go in the library |\r\n\r\nNeuron records (`graph/neurons/`), `graph/edges.json` and `graph/index.json` are **derived** by\r\n`python -m bonsai_interp.graph.fold`. Nobody edits them by hand.\r\n\r\n## 2. In the library: bulky, optional, referenced by hash\r\n\r\nPut large or numerous artefacts in the library and list them in your contribution's `artifacts`:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n\"artifacts\": [{\"name\": \"bank.json\", \"url\": \"https://huggingface.co/datasets/<org>/bonsai-interp-library/resolve/main/<id>/bank.json\",\r\n               \"sha256\": \"â€¦\", \"bytes\": 174612}]\r\n```\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n- sentence banks larger than the test sets\r\n- inverse-solver evaluation logs\r\n- band samples\r\n- steering generations\r\n- clean-room transcripts and room bundles\r\n- HTML dossiers and figures\r\n\r\nThe library is a Hugging Face dataset repository: versioned, built for large files, and free.\r\nThe merge agent checks that each URL resolves and that its hash matches. A missing library file\r\nnever invalidates a contribution, because git already holds the evidence.\r\n\r\n## 3. Nowhere\r\n\r\n- **Activation vectors, tensors, scans or caches.** Recompute them from the recipe. If one is\r\n  essential to a claim, upload it to the library and explain why.\r\n- **Model weights, or copies of the corpus.** They are on Hugging Face and in the `data-v1`\r\n  release.\r\n- **Secrets** (`.env`, API keys, tokens), **local paths**, and personal data about anyone.\r\n- **Long copied text**: corpus excerpts over 400 characters, or third-party documents.\r\n\r\n## Why this split\r\n\r\n- **Reproducible:** every number in git has a recipe. Anyone with the model can regenerate the\r\n  bulky parts, so storing them is a convenience, not a requirement.\r\n- **Reviewable:** a PR is a few kilobytes of JSON that a person or an agent can read in full.\r\n- **Bounded:** at about 16 KB per quick-tested neuron, 10,000 neurons is about 150 MB of git.\r\n  The library grows without slowing anyone's clone.\r\n<!-- PR shape for a SWEEP: many poles, one pre-registration commit, one batch run.\r\n     Title: \"sweep: 16 poles (<held> held)\". `play run` prints the table rows below; paste them. -->\r\n\r\n## Sweep: <!-- N --> poles, <!-- held --> held\r\n\r\n- **Agent:** <!-- e.g. claude-code opus-5.5, codex, human -->\r\n- **Pre-registration commit:** <!-- hash: every tests file, committed together and alone -->\r\n- **Results commit:** <!-- hash, a LATER commit -->\r\n- **Explored before pre-registering:** <!-- e.g. cards only / probe --plan on 10 poles -->\r\n\r\n| pole | label | unit kind | AUROC | median fire | near misses â‰¥ 0.30 | outcome |\r\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\r\n| <!-- bonsai1/L__/N____Â± --> | <!-- short label --> | <!-- lexical / contextual / â€¦ --> | | | | <!-- held / failed --> |\r\n\r\n## Notes\r\n\r\n<!-- anything a reviewer should know: poles marked dense / positional, labels you are unsure of, decisions you made -->\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [ ] Only contribution files are added (a tests file and a results file per pole, under `graph/contributions/â€¦`). No derived files.\r\n- [ ] The tests files were committed together, before any of them was run, and nothing was re-tested after seeing results.\r\n- [ ] Near misses keep the topic, length and style and drop only the claimed trigger.\r\n- [ ] Failed labels are included, not dropped.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
